\textbf{Abstract:} A lifelong reinforcement learning agent is characterized by its ability to acquire new skills without forgetting previously acquired knowledge. This paper presents a lifelong reinforcement learning method that exploits the inherent structure of state-action transition probabilities. Specifically, we employ a mixture of Gaussian processes to model the transition function, where each Gaussian process is conditioned on a particular task. This strategy allows the agent to capture the underlying structure of the transition function, resulting in the efficient acquisition of new tasks. We evaluate our approach on gridworld tasks, where an agent is required to learn a sequence of increasingly complex tasks. Our results indicate that the proposed method surpasses existing methods for lifelong reinforcement learning. Additionally, we show that the method is compatible with any reinforcement learning algorithm that utilizes a transition function model. The source code is available at https://github.com/abhishek1995/LifelongRL.
