\section*{Introduction}
Reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved remarkable success in various domains, including robotics, video games, and autonomous driving. However, most RL algorithms suffer from sample inefficiency, requiring extensive environmental interactions to learn effective policies. This limitation is especially problematic in high-dimensional continuous control scenarios, where the vast action space and complex dynamics make learning difficult. To tackle this, we propose a novel solution that combines a learned dynamics model with a learned reward model. By training a latent reward model, we can estimate rewards for unseen observations, thereby facilitating the learning of a robust policy. Our method demonstrates substantial improvements in sample efficiency across a range of continuous control tasks. Additionally, we illustrate that the latent reward model can be leveraged to recover the observation space, allowing the learned policy to be applied to real-world environments.

\section*{Related Work}
Reinforcement learning has been extensively researched and applied to numerous domains, including robotics, autonomous driving, and video games. A primary challenge in RL is sample efficiency, which is exacerbated in high-dimensional continuous control problems. To mitigate this, researchers have developed model-based RL algorithms that utilize learned dynamics models to simulate environments and guide policy learning. Our approach builds upon this by introducing a latent reward model that projects latent states to the reward space, thereby improving the robustness of the learned policy. Furthermore, we show that the latent reward model can be used to recover the observation space, enabling the application of the learned policy to real-world tasks.

